"The postoperative pathology demonstrated a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pathological T2 stage with negative surgical margins. Reassuring findings include the absence of extraprostatic extension, no invasion of the seminal vesicles, and no lymphovascular invasion, as noted in the surgical pathology report: \"The robot-assisted radical prostatectomy specimen showed Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), pathological stage pT2. There was no extraprostatic extension; surgical margins were negative; the seminal vesicles were not invaded; lymphovascular invasion was absent; no lymph nodes were removed.\" The biopsy identified Acinar adenocarcinoma. A significant factor limiting prognosis is the lack of nodal sampling, as the report states, \"No lymph nodes were removed,\" which means pNx does not establish pN0 status, widening uncertainty regarding regional disease control. Countervailing findings are the negative margins and absence of seminal vesicle invasion. Postoperative PSA is not recorded, meaning biochemical recurrence cannot be assessed. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its trajectory would be crucial to revise the prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 52.24 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 33.95–70.52 months; its coverage has not been validated."
